# IDE
.idea/

# SDD playbook (process docs are kept outside the tracked repository)
SDD/

# Per-user Claude Code overrides. `.claude/settings.json` and `.claude/hooks/`
# ARE tracked — they carry project gates that must apply to every agent.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Worktrees, both kinds. A worktree inside the repo it is a worktree of is an
# embedded repository, and `git add -A` commits it as a **gitlink**: a pointer to
# a repository no clone can resolve, which checks out as an empty directory with
# `git status` clean and the build green. git only WARNS — an embedded repo is
# never an error — so no exit code catches it. It happened in the research repo
# on 2026-08-03 (two lane worktrees, one commit) and the fix landed only there;
# these two lines are the same fix on this side.
#   /wt-*/            lane worktrees, which belong BESIDE this repo, not in it
#   .claude/worktrees/  made by Claude Code's own agent isolation, without asking
/wt-*/
.claude/worktrees/

# Game install and extracted asset bytes — never tracked, never released.
# The check-no-game-assets.sh guard is the enforcing backstop; these entries
# keep the common cases from being staged by accident in the first place.
/game/
examples/samples/
*.res
*.reg
*.alm
*.16a
*.256
*.spr
*.pal
*.fnt
*.fon
*.wav
*.snd
*.ogg
*.mp3
*.smk
*.bik
*.mpg
*.avi
*.exe
*.dll

# Decoded sprite PNGs from cmd/sprtool are converted game assets — never track
# them. The asset guard does not key on .png, so this ignore is the backstop;
# point `sprtool png` at this folder (or any path outside the repo).
/sprtool-out/

# Rendered terrain PNGs from cmd/terraintool are likewise converted game assets;
# point `terraintool render -out` at this folder (or any path outside the repo).
/terraintool-out/

# Rendered text PNGs from cmd/texttool are converted game assets — glyph art out
# of the shipped font atlases. `render -out` has no default path, so a picture is
# only ever written where a caller names; this is the backstop for a caller who
# names somewhere inside the tree.
/texttool-out/

# Build output
/bin/
/.gocache/
/logs/

# Saved games. A `go run` launch writes them beside the module it was run from
# (pkg/game.DefaultSaveDir), which is this directory — and a save carries a
# marshalled world built out of a lawful install's map. The asset guard keys on
# extensions and would not catch `.ags`, so this entry is the backstop that keeps
# game-derived bytes from being staged by accident.
/saves/

# Local player preferences written by the game beside the working-directory
# save root (for example GameSpeed). Keep the owner's live settings out of Git.
/options.txt

# The Hall of Fame the game writes to the same player profile when a campaign
# ends. It is the owner's play record, never a repository file.
/famehall.dat

# Per-story runnable builds (see AGENTS.md "Process (SDD)"): one directory per
# story under builds/<NNNN>-<slug>/, holding the built binary and a run README.
# Binaries and any converted-asset output they produce are never tracked.
/builds/

# A lane's handoff note. `HANDOFF.md` is state about a WORKTREE -- its branch,
# its fork point, its submodule pin, the version it took -- and every line of it
# is false the moment that worktree is torn down. One reached master on 2026-07-
# xx inside `0089`'s verification commit and sat at the repo root for 24 stories
# describing a branch, a pin and a format version that no longer existed, in the
# imperative, as if it were work still owed. The owner found it and said what it
# was: a file that lies.
#
# A lane's handoff belongs in its RETURN TEXT, or -- when it must survive the
# lane -- in that story's own `docs/<NNNN>/`, where it is dated by the story it
# sits in and nobody mistakes it for a live instruction.
/HANDOFF.md
